\ ff-test: lines budget=1000000
\ A definition compiles into the word its `:` made, from `:` to `;`, and
\ an error that ends the line abandons it.
\
\ The compiler used to write into whichever word was newest, so a word
\ made between `[` and `]` took over the rest of the definition, and a
\ failed definition stayed in the dictionary half-compiled, with no EXIT:
\ calling it ran off the end of its body.

: p 32 emit . ;

\ A failed definition leaves nothing behind, and the next line runs
\ instead of compiling into the wreck.
: bad 1 2 zork ;
: good bad ;
3 4 + p cr

\ Any error before `;` does it: one in code run between `[` and `]`, or
\ a stray token where the name should be.
: bad2 [ 1 0 / ] ;
bad2
: 42
6 p cr

\ An error that is caught before it reaches the definition doesn't:
\ `evaluate` pushes its code, and compilation carries on.
: ok1 [ "zork" evaluate ] literal ;
ok1 p cr

\ A definition begun in evaluated source goes with that source's error.
"  : bad3 zork ;" evaluate p cr
bad3

\ Definitions don't nest; the outer one is abandoned too.
: outer [ : inner ] ;
outer
inner

\ A word made between `[` and `]` has storage of its own and leaves the
\ rest of the definition alone.
: holder 1 [ variable spot ] 2 + ;
holder p spot @ p cr

\ `forget` can't pull the open definition out from under the compiler.
: doomed [ forget p ] ;
5 p cr

\ `]` resumes the open definition.
: later [ 6 ] literal ;
later p cr

\ A word can call itself by name even as its first token, which used to
\ compile a call through a garbage pointer.
: forever forever ;
see forever

\ So redefining a word in terms of its old self recurses into the new
\ one: here out of return stack, reported rather than crashing.
: again-word 1 ;
: again-word again-word 1 + ;
again-word

\ `recurse` is the standard spelling.
: fact dup 1 > if dup 1 - recurse * then ;
5 fact p cr
